red-team mode — new mode contribution

WHAT
====
A domain-agnostic adversarial-review mode. It treats the proposal itself
(architecture, code change, plan, strategy, prompt, process, contract clause)
as the target: assumption register -> attack tree -> FMEA/pre-mortem ->
weakest link and seams -> research validation -> constructive counter-proposals
-> verdict HARDEN / REDESIGN / ACCEPT WITH CONTROLS / SOUND.

WHY IT IS NOT A DUPLICATE
=========================
Nearest existing modes and why they do not cover this:
  penetration-tester      hands-on, authorised security exploitation only
  security-auditor        compliance/control scope
  code-reviewer           code artefacts only
  bullshit-detection-analyst  truth of claims, not survivability of a proposal
  anti-fiction-sentinel   success/verification claims
  cognitive-multi-thinker adversarial thinking as one lens among many
None takes an arbitrary proposal as the systematic target of a constructive
attack that must end in options rather than a verdict alone.

FILES AND PLACEMENT
===================
  agents/meta-orchestration/general/red-team.yaml
      flat agent schema — the same key set used by the other 233 files under
      agents/ (slug, name, category, subcategory, description, roleDefinition,
      whenToUse, groups, version, lastUpdated, customInstructions).
      Category meta-orchestration is the home of its closest sibling,
      bullshit-detection-analyst. CONTRIBUTING.md is followed literally:
      agents/<category>/<subcategory>/<slug>.yaml plus a custom_modes.yaml entry.
  custom_modes.yaml
      matching entry appended; file now holds 306 modes.

VALIDATION (performed)
======================
  python3 scripts/validate_custom_modes.py custom_modes.yaml  -> pass, 306 modes
  parity asserted in a script: the agents/ file and the custom_modes.yaml
  entry parse to identical slug, name, description, roleDefinition,
  whenToUse, groups and customInstructions values (no drift between mirror
  and compiled copy).
  python3 -c 'yaml.safe_load(open(...))' on both files -> OK
  all cross-referenced modes exist upstream: bullshit-detection-analyst,
  penetration-tester, code-reviewer, security-auditor, architect-reviewer,
  anti-fiction-sentinel, risk-manager (each found exactly once under
  custom_modes.d/ and under agents/).

KNOWN LIMITATIONS, STATED UP FRONT
==================================
1. validate_custom_modes.py exits 1 for any flat agents/ document because
   validate_document() requires customModes as the ONLY top-level key:
       validation error: Top-level contains unsupported keys: [...]
   This is not specific to this file — the same command exits 1 for all 233
   existing agents/ files (verified on ui-expert, code-reviewer,
   bullshit-detection-analyst, anti-fiction-sentinel,
   edge-computing-architect). The custom_modes.yaml entry, which is the form
   the validator actually supports, passes.
2. The mode is NOT added to .roomodes because CONTRIBUTING.md does not ask
   for that; python3 scripts/generate_roomodes.py will include it if the
   compiled set should ship it.
3. Formatting: roleDefinition/whenToUse/customInstructions use literal block
   scalars to preserve the text byte for byte, which means some lines exceed
   the 100-character guideline in CONTRIBUTING; wrapping them would insert
   newlines into the prompt text.

